ic OS","\u002Fplatform","04.platform",[174,179,183,188,192,197,201,206,211,216,221,226,248,253,258],{"title":175,"path":176,"stem":177,"icon":178},"System Architecture","\u002Fplatform\u002Farchitecture","04.platform\u002F01.architecture","i-lucide-boxes",{"title":180,"path":181,"stem":182,"icon":6},"Deployment Guide","\u002Fplatform\u002Fdeployment-guide","04.platform\u002F02.deployment-guide",{"title":184,"path":185,"stem":186,"icon":187},"Configuration","\u002Fplatform\u002Fconfiguration","04.platform\u002F03.configuration","i-lucide-settings",{"title":189,"path":190,"stem":191,"icon":21},"Organization Management","\u002Fplatform\u002Forganization-management","04.platform\u002F04.organization-management",{"title":193,"path":194,"stem":195,"icon":196},"System Security","\u002Fplatform\u002Fsystem-security","04.platform\u002F05.system-security","i-lucide-shield",{"title":198,"path":199,"stem":200,"icon":38},"Defense in Depth","\u002Fplatform\u002Fdefense-in-depth","04.platform\u002F06.defense-in-depth",{"title":202,"path":203,"stem":204,"icon":205},"MCP Tools","\u002Fplatform\u002Fmcp-tools","04.platform\u002F07.mcp-tools","i-lucide-bot",{"title":207,"path":208,"stem":209,"icon":210},"Observability","\u002Fplatform\u002Fobservability","04.platform\u002F08.observability","i-lucide-activity",{"title":212,"path":213,"stem":214,"icon":215},"Contributing","\u002Fplatform\u002Fcontributing","04.platform\u002F09.contributing","i-lucide-git-pull-request",{"title":217,"path":218,"stem":219,"icon":220},"System Migrations","\u002Fplatform\u002Fsystem-migrations","04.platform\u002F10.system-migrations","i-lucide-database-zap",{"title":222,"path":223,"stem":224,"icon":225},"Service Call Liveness","\u002Fplatform\u002Fservice-call-liveness","04.platform\u002F11.service-call-liveness","i-lucide-heart-pulse",{"title":155,"icon":156,"path":227,"stem":228,"children":229,"page":18},"\u002Fplatform\u002Freference","04.platform\u002F12.reference",[230,235,240,244],{"title":231,"path":232,"stem":233,"icon":234},"CRI Format","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fcri-format","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F01.cri-format","i-lucide-link",{"title":236,"path":237,"stem":238,"icon":239},"Grind Jobs","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fgrind-jobs","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F02.grind-jobs","i-lucide-workflow",{"title":241,"path":242,"stem":243,"icon":178},"Project Publishing Design","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fproject-publishing-design","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F03.project-publishing-design",{"title":245,"path":246,"stem":247,"icon":33},"Sign-In Design","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fsign-in-design","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F04.sign-in-design",{"title":249,"path":250,"stem":251,"icon":252},"Development Server","\u002Fplatform\u002Fdevelopment-server","04.platform\u002F13.development-server","i-lucide-server-cog",{"title":254,"path":255,"stem":256,"icon":257},"Supply Chain Security","\u002Fplatform\u002Fsupply-chain-security","04.platform\u002F14.supply-chain-security","i-lucide-package-check",{"title":259,"path":260,"stem":261,"icon":262},"Enterprise License","\u002Fplatform\u002Fenterprise-license","04.platform\u002F15.enterprise-license","i-lucide-badge-check",{"id":264,"title":217,"body":265,"description":881,"extension":882,"links":883,"meta":884,"navigation":885,"path":218,"seo":886,"stem":219,"__hash__":887},"docs\u002F04.platform\u002F10.system-migrations.md",{"type":266,"value":267,"toc":868},"minimark",[268,277,282,285,297,301,309,313,316,332,337,340,393,407,512,515,530,536,540,555,565,569,583,613,629,637,657,675,722,736,747,781,799,829,845,849,864],[269,270,271,272,276],"p",{},"The Kinotic platform uses the same migration SQL system as application developers to manage its own internal Elasticsearch indices. This page covers considerations specific to platform development — for general migration syntax, see ",[273,274,275],"a",{"href":118},"App Migrations",".",[278,279,281],"h2",{"id":280},"what-are-system-migrations","What Are System Migrations?",[269,283,284],{},"System migrations define and evolve the indices that the platform itself relies on — things like migration history tracking, system configuration, and other internal data stores. They run during platform startup before any application migrations and are recorded under the system organization and project rather than under an organization's project. Migration history is keyed by organization and project, since a project id is unique only within its organization.",[269,286,287,288,292,293,296],{},"System migrations live in ",[289,290,291],"code",{},"kinotic-migration\u002Fsrc\u002Fmain\u002Fresources\u002Fmigrations\u002F"," and follow the same ",[289,294,295],{},"V\u003CN>__\u003Cdescription>.sql"," naming convention.",[278,298,300],{"id":299},"using-composite-types-in-system-indices","Using Composite Types in System Indices",[269,302,303,304,308],{},"OBJECT, NESTED, and UNION columns work the same way in system migrations as in application migrations. Refer to the ",[273,305,307],{"href":306},"\u002Fapps\u002Fpersistence\u002Fmigrations#composite-column-types","Composite Column Types"," section of the app documentation for syntax and examples.",[278,310,312],{"id":311},"deserializing-union-types","Deserializing Union Types",[269,314,315],{},"Elasticsearch stores UNION fields as a flat merged object — it has no knowledge of which variant a document contains. When reading documents back into Java, the platform needs to route deserialization to the correct subclass.",[269,317,318,319,323,324,327,328,331],{},"The standard approach is Jackson's polymorphic type handling, driven by a ",[320,321,322],"strong",{},"discriminator field"," stored in the document. This is the same ",[289,325,326],{},"kind"," (or similar) ",[289,329,330],{},"KEYWORD"," field you include in each union variant in the migration SQL.",[333,334,336],"h3",{"id":335},"example","Example",[269,338,339],{},"Given this migration:",[341,342,347],"pre",{"className":343,"code":344,"language":345,"meta":346,"style":346},"language-sql shiki shiki-themes material-theme-lighter material-theme material-theme-palenight","CREATE TABLE assets (\n    id KEYWORD,\n    item UNION (\n        Book  (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, isbn KEYWORD),\n        Video (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, duration INTEGER)\n    )\n);\n","sql","",[289,348,349,357,363,369,375,381,387],{"__ignoreMap":346},[350,351,354],"span",{"class":352,"line":353},"line",1,[350,355,356],{},"CREATE TABLE assets (\n",[350,358,360],{"class":352,"line":359},2,[350,361,362],{},"    id KEYWORD,\n",[350,364,366],{"class":352,"line":365},3,[350,367,368],{},"    item UNION (\n",[350,370,372],{"class":352,"line":371},4,[350,373,374],{},"        Book  (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, isbn KEYWORD),\n",[350,376,378],{"class":352,"line":377},5,[350,379,380],{},"        Video (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, duration INTEGER)\n",[350,382,384],{"class":352,"line":383},6,[350,385,386],{},"    )\n",[350,388,390],{"class":352,"line":389},7,[350,391,392],{},");\n",[269,394,395,396,399,400,403,404,406],{},"The corresponding Java model uses ",[289,397,398],{},"@JsonTypeInfo"," and ",[289,401,402],{},"@JsonSubTypes"," to map the ",[289,405,326],{}," field value to the correct subclass:",[341,408,412],{"className":409,"code":410,"language":411,"meta":346,"style":346},"language-java shiki shiki-themes material-theme-lighter material-theme material-theme-palenight","@JsonTypeInfo(use = JsonTypeInfo.Id.NAME, property = \"kind\")\n@JsonSubTypes({\n    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Book.class,  name = \"Book\"),\n    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Video.class, name = \"Video\")\n})\npublic abstract class Item {}\n\npublic class Book extends Item {\n    public String kind;\n    public String title;\n    public String isbn;\n}\n\npublic class Video extends Item {\n    public String kind;\n    public String title;\n    public Integer duration;\n}\n","java",[289,413,414,419,424,429,434,439,444,450,456,462,468,474,480,485,491,496,501,507],{"__ignoreMap":346},[350,415,416],{"class":352,"line":353},[350,417,418],{},"@JsonTypeInfo(use = JsonTypeInfo.Id.NAME, property = \"kind\")\n",[350,420,421],{"class":352,"line":359},[350,422,423],{},"@JsonSubTypes({\n",[350,425,426],{"class":352,"line":365},[350,427,428],{},"    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Book.class,  name = \"Book\"),\n",[350,430,431],{"class":352,"line":371},[350,432,433],{},"    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Video.class, name = \"Video\")\n",[350,435,436],{"class":352,"line":377},[350,437,438],{},"})\n",[350,440,441],{"class":352,"line":383},[350,442,443],{},"public abstract class Item {}\n",[350,445,446],{"class":352,"line":389},[350,447,449],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":448},true,"\n",[350,451,453],{"class":352,"line":452},8,[350,454,455],{},"public class Book extends Item {\n",[350,457,459],{"class":352,"line":458},9,[350,460,461],{},"    public String kind;\n",[350,463,465],{"class":352,"line":464},10,[350,466,467],{},"    public String title;\n",[350,469,471],{"class":352,"line":470},11,[350,472,473],{},"    public String isbn;\n",[350,475,477],{"class":352,"line":476},12,[350,478,479],{},"}\n",[350,481,483],{"class":352,"line":482},13,[350,484,449],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":448},[350,486,488],{"class":352,"line":487},14,[350,489,490],{},"public class Video extends Item {\n",[350,492,494],{"class":352,"line":493},15,[350,495,461],{},[350,497,499],{"class":352,"line":498},16,[350,500,467],{},[350,502,504],{"class":352,"line":503},17,[350,505,506],{},"    public Integer duration;\n",[350,508,510],{"class":352,"line":509},18,[350,511,479],{},[269,513,514],{},"When inserting documents, always write the discriminator value:",[341,516,518],{"className":343,"code":517,"language":345,"meta":346,"style":346},"INSERT INTO assets (id, item)\n    VALUES ('a1', { kind: 'Book', title: 'Clean Code', isbn: '978-0132350884' }) WITH REFRESH;\n",[289,519,520,525],{"__ignoreMap":346},[350,521,522],{"class":352,"line":353},[350,523,524],{},"INSERT INTO assets (id, item)\n",[350,526,527],{"class":352,"line":359},[350,528,529],{},"    VALUES ('a1', { kind: 'Book', title: 'Clean Code', isbn: '978-0132350884' }) WITH REFRESH;\n",[269,531,532,533,535],{},"If the ",[289,534,326],{}," field is absent from a stored document, Jackson will fail to deserialize it. Ensure every INSERT that targets a UNION field includes the discriminator.",[333,537,539],{"id":538},"conflict-validation","Conflict validation",[269,541,542,543,546,547,550,551,554],{},"If two variants declare the same field name with different scalar types — e.g. ",[289,544,545],{},"title TEXT"," in one variant and ",[289,548,549],{},"title KEYWORD"," in another — the migration executor will throw an ",[289,552,553],{},"IllegalArgumentException"," at mapping time. Fields shared across variants must have identical types.",[269,556,557,560,561,564],{},[320,558,559],{},"Known limitation:"," Conflict detection is shallow for composite types. If two variants share a field name with the same composite type (OBJECT, NESTED, or UNION) but different sub-fields, no exception is thrown — only the first variant's sub-field definitions are used and the second's are silently dropped. Until named type support is added to the migration DSL, avoid sharing composite-typed fields across variants. Scalar shared fields (such as a ",[289,562,563],{},"kind KEYWORD"," discriminator) are always safe.",[278,566,568],{"id":567},"data-streams","Data Streams",[269,570,571,572,575,576,582],{},"System data that is append-only and time-series — audit trails, events, telemetry — should be backed by an Elasticsearch ",[320,573,574],{},"data stream"," rather than a regular index, so old data ages out and backing indices roll over automatically. Create one from a migration with ",[273,577,579],{"href":578},"\u002Fapps\u002Freference\u002Fmigration-sql-grammar#create-data-stream",[289,580,581],{},"CREATE DATA STREAM",":",[341,584,586],{"className":343,"code":585,"language":345,"meta":346,"style":346},"CREATE DATA STREAM system_audit (\n    actor  KEYWORD,\n    action KEYWORD,\n    detail TEXT\n) WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '90d') ;\n",[289,587,588,593,598,603,608],{"__ignoreMap":346},[350,589,590],{"class":352,"line":353},[350,591,592],{},"CREATE DATA STREAM system_audit (\n",[350,594,595],{"class":352,"line":359},[350,596,597],{},"    actor  KEYWORD,\n",[350,599,600],{"class":352,"line":365},[350,601,602],{},"    action KEYWORD,\n",[350,604,605],{"class":352,"line":371},[350,606,607],{},"    detail TEXT\n",[350,609,610],{"class":352,"line":377},[350,611,612],{},") WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '90d') ;\n",[269,614,615,618,619,625,626,628],{},[289,616,617],{},"DATA_RETENTION"," is the native ",[273,620,624],{"href":621,"rel":622},"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.elastic.co\u002Fdocs\u002Fmanage-data\u002Flifecycle\u002Fdata-stream",[623],"nofollow","data stream lifecycle"," — Elasticsearch deletes data past the period (measured per backing index from rollover, not per document). See the ",[273,627,166],{"href":578}," for the full statement and the exact retention semantics.",[333,630,632,633,636],{"id":631},"every-document-needs-a-timestamp-and-java-needs-ceremony-for-it","Every document needs a ",[289,634,635],{},"@timestamp"," — and Java needs ceremony for it",[269,638,639,640,643,644,646,647,650,651,653,654,656],{},"Elasticsearch ",[320,641,642],{},"requires"," every document written to a data stream to carry a ",[289,645,635],{}," date field, and rejects any that don't with ",[289,648,649],{},"data stream timestamp field [@timestamp] is missing",". The migration adds ",[289,652,635],{}," to the mapping automatically — you neither declare it as a column nor can you, since ",[289,655,635],{}," is not a legal column identifier.",[269,658,659,660,663,664,666,667,670,671,674],{},"The catch is on the ",[320,661,662],{},"write"," side: ",[289,665,635],{}," is also not a legal Java field name, so a system DTO cannot carry it directly. Bridge it with Jackson's ",[289,668,669],{},"@JsonProperty"," — the ",[289,672,673],{},"ElasticsearchAsyncClient"," serializes through a Jackson mapper, so the annotation is honored on both write and read:",[341,676,678],{"className":409,"code":677,"language":411,"meta":346,"style":346},"public class AuditEvent {\n    private String  id;\n\n    @JsonProperty(\"@timestamp\")      \u002F\u002F Java field `timestamp` ⇄ Elasticsearch field `@timestamp`\n    private Instant timestamp;\n\n    private String  actor;\n    private String  action;\n}\n",[289,679,680,685,690,694,699,704,708,713,718],{"__ignoreMap":346},[350,681,682],{"class":352,"line":353},[350,683,684],{},"public class AuditEvent {\n",[350,686,687],{"class":352,"line":359},[350,688,689],{},"    private String  id;\n",[350,691,692],{"class":352,"line":365},[350,693,449],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":448},[350,695,696],{"class":352,"line":371},[350,697,698],{},"    @JsonProperty(\"@timestamp\")      \u002F\u002F Java field `timestamp` ⇄ Elasticsearch field `@timestamp`\n",[350,700,701],{"class":352,"line":377},[350,702,703],{},"    private Instant timestamp;\n",[350,705,706],{"class":352,"line":383},[350,707,449],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":448},[350,709,710],{"class":352,"line":389},[350,711,712],{},"    private String  actor;\n",[350,714,715],{"class":352,"line":452},[350,716,717],{},"    private String  action;\n",[350,719,720],{"class":352,"line":458},[350,721,479],{},[269,723,724,725,728,729,731,732,735],{},"This ",[320,726,727],{},"renames"," the field — the stored document has only ",[289,730,635],{},", so any query or sort must use that literal name (",[289,733,734],{},"timestamp"," does not exist in the index). For most internal stream DTOs this is the simplest correct choice.",[269,737,738,739,743,744,746],{},"If you instead want a friendly, separately-queryable field name ",[740,741,742],"em",{},"alongside"," ",[289,745,635],{},", keep your own field and add a serialize-only companion:",[341,748,750],{"className":409,"code":749,"language":411,"meta":346,"style":346},"@JsonProperty(\"eventTime\")           \u002F\u002F stays queryable as eventTime\nprivate Instant eventTime;\n\n\u002F\u002F emitted as @timestamp on write, ignored on read (eventTime is restored from its own key)\n@JsonProperty(value = \"@timestamp\", access = JsonProperty.Access.READ_ONLY)\npublic Instant esTimestamp() { return eventTime; }\n",[289,751,752,757,762,766,771,776],{"__ignoreMap":346},[350,753,754],{"class":352,"line":353},[350,755,756],{},"@JsonProperty(\"eventTime\")           \u002F\u002F stays queryable as eventTime\n",[350,758,759],{"class":352,"line":359},[350,760,761],{},"private Instant eventTime;\n",[350,763,764],{"class":352,"line":365},[350,765,449],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":448},[350,767,768],{"class":352,"line":371},[350,769,770],{},"\u002F\u002F emitted as @timestamp on write, ignored on read (eventTime is restored from its own key)\n",[350,772,773],{"class":352,"line":377},[350,774,775],{},"@JsonProperty(value = \"@timestamp\", access = JsonProperty.Access.READ_ONLY)\n",[350,777,778],{"class":352,"line":383},[350,779,780],{},"public Instant esTimestamp() { return eventTime; }\n",[269,782,724,783,786,787,399,790,792,793,798],{},[320,784,785],{},"duplicates"," the value (both ",[289,788,789],{},"eventTime",[289,791,635],{}," are stored). It is the same document shape that application entities get from the ",[273,794,795],{"href":102},[289,796,797],{},"@TimeReference"," decorator. When a migration creates the stream backing such an entity, declare the pairing so the stream documents which column is the natural time field:",[341,800,802],{"className":343,"code":801,"language":345,"meta":346,"style":346},"CREATE DATA STREAM sensor_readings (\n    id        KEYWORD,\n    eventTime DATE,\n    value     DOUBLE\n) WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '30d', TIME_REFERENCE = 'eventTime') ;\n",[289,803,804,809,814,819,824],{"__ignoreMap":346},[350,805,806],{"class":352,"line":353},[350,807,808],{},"CREATE DATA STREAM sensor_readings (\n",[350,810,811],{"class":352,"line":359},[350,812,813],{},"    id        KEYWORD,\n",[350,815,816],{"class":352,"line":365},[350,817,818],{},"    eventTime DATE,\n",[350,820,821],{"class":352,"line":371},[350,822,823],{},"    value     DOUBLE\n",[350,825,826],{"class":352,"line":377},[350,827,828],{},") WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '30d', TIME_REFERENCE = 'eventTime') ;\n",[269,830,831,834,835,838,839,841,842,844],{},[289,832,833],{},"TIME_REFERENCE"," must name a declared ",[289,836,837],{},"DATE"," column; the migration validates this and fails fast otherwise. It declares intent and pairs with the entity's ",[289,840,797],{}," — the write-time duplication into ",[289,843,635],{}," is performed by the persistence layer, not the migration.",[333,846,848],{"id":847},"writing-and-reading","Writing and reading",[269,850,851,852,855,856,859,860,863],{},"Data streams are ",[320,853,854],{},"append-only",": Elasticsearch accepts only the ",[289,857,858],{},"create"," op-type and rejects the ",[289,861,862],{},"index"," op and by-id updates\u002Fdeletes. Documents are retrieved by search\u002Fquery, not by id. System services that write to a stream must therefore use a create (append) write, and read through search rather than a find-by-id lookup.",[865,866,867],"style",{},"html .light .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-light);background: var(--shiki-light-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-light-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-light-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-light-text-decoration);}html.light .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-light);background: var(--shiki-light-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-light-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-light-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-light-text-decoration);}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);